  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/taia_naticoides_en/",
      "html": "<p>A live-bearing snail imported from Inle Lake in Burma. It is a close relative of the European river snails (Viviparus). Adults may reach about 35 mm. Suggested common name: Nubby piano snail.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/taia_naticoides_en/",
      "html": "<p>Inle Lake, Burma.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/taia_naticoides_en/",
      "html": "<p>As they come from subtropical regions they are suited to a broad array of temperatures and can be kept between 15 and 28°C. It is a very slow and calm species of snail. Like many other river snails they are sensitive to bad water conditions, so their aquarium should be kept with the same attention as when keeping fish.</p><p>The species likes to bury itself from time to time, so at least a part of the aquarium bottom should be of fine, soft sand.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/taia_naticoides_en/",
      "html": "<p>Like the European river snails, they feed on soft particles such as mouldered food tablets and detritus. They are not suited to combating algae, but they leave plants completely unharmed.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/taia_naticoides_en/",
      "html": "<p>Males and females exist. The sexes can be easily distinguished by the broad and curled right antenna of the male. Coloration and shell shape cannot be used to tell the sexes apart, as both are very variable. Although in general only the attractive black-and-white specimens are traded, they can also be uniformly brown.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/taia_naticoides_en/",
      "html": "<p>The young are about 2–4 mm in height when born. It seems that the females can produce offspring of different sizes.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/taia_naticoides_en/",
      "html": "<p>Lexicon: Taia: not explained in the original description; the word has very different meanings in Latin, ancient Greek and Sanskrit. It may also be an indigenous name for snails at Inle Lake. naticoides: means \"similar to naticus\". viviparus: Latin, means \"livebearing\".</p>"
    }
  ],
